{"id":"b004c38d-fc78-44c4-aa8c-13696a9feb13","object":"chat.completion","created":1785031754,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n\\n    # Extract fields\\n    passing = case.get('passing')\\n    failing = case.get('failing')\\n    required_diff = case.get('required_difference')\\n    unresolved_diff = case.get('unresolved_difference')\\n\\n    # Validate types and presence\\n    if not isinstance(passing, list) or not isinstance(failing, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"passing and failing must be lists\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(required_diff, list) or not isinstance(unresolved_diff, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"required_difference and unresolved_difference must be lists\\\")\\n    for lst, name in [(passing, 'passing'), (failing, 'failing'), (required_diff, 'required_difference'), (unresolved_diff, 'unresolved_difference')]:\\n        if not all(isinstance(x, str) for x in lst):\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"All elements in {name} must be strings\\\")\\n\\n    # Compute the ordered difference (failing - passing)\\n    pass_set = set(passing)\\n    diff = [x for x in failing if x not in pass_set]  # preserve order\\n\\n    # Validate that required_diff and unresolved_diff are subsets of diff\\n    required_set = set(required_diff)\\n    unresolved_set = set(unresolved_diff)\\n    if not required_set.issubset(diff):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"required_difference must be a subset of the failing-passing difference\\\")\\n    if not unresolved_set.issubset(diff):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"unresolved_difference must be a subset of the failing-passing difference\\\")\\n    if required_set & unresolved_set:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"required_difference and unresolved_difference must be disjoint\\\")\\n\\n    # If diff is empty, no isolation needed\\n    if not diff:\\n        return {'isolated_difference': [], 'tests': 0}\\n\\n    test_count = 0\\n\\n    def test(subset):\\n        nonlocal test_count\\n        test_count += 1\\n        subset_set = set(subset)\\n        # If any unresolved element present -> UNRESOLVED\\n        if unresolved_set & subset_set:\\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        # If all required elements present -> FAIL\\n        if required_set.issubset(subset_set):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n\\n    # ddmin algorithm\\n    def ddmin(c):\\n        n = 2\\n        while True:\\n            subsets = []\\n            # Partition c into n contiguous parts (preserve order)\\n            length = len(c)\\n            if n > length:\\n                n = length\\n            # Calculate equal sized partitions, remainder distributed\\n            base = length // n\\n            rem = length % n\\n            start = 0\\n            for i in range(n):\\n                size = base + (1 if i < rem else 0)\\n                subsets.append(c[start:start+size])\\n                start += size\\n\\n            reduced = False\\n            for subset in subsets:\\n                res = test(subset)\\n                if res == 'FAIL':\\n                    c = subset\\n                    n = 2\\n                    reduced = True\\n                    break\\n                # complement = c without subset (preserve order)\\n                comp = [x for x in c if x not in subset]\\n                # For complement we also need to check for FAIL\\n                res_comp = test(comp)\\n                if res_comp == 'FAIL':\\n                    c = comp\\n                    n = 2\\n                    reduced = True\\n                    break\\n            if not reduced:\\n                if n < len(c):\\n                    n = min(2 * n, len(c))\\n                else:\\n                    break\\n        return c\\n\\n    isolated = ddmin(diff)\\n    return {'isolated_difference': isolated, 'tests': test_count}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a Python function solve(case) that implements delta debugging (ddmin) to isolate a minimal difference between passing and failing sets. The case payload has: passing (list of strings), failing (list of strings), required_difference (list of strings), unresolved_difference (list of strings). The goal: find a minimal subset of the difference (failing - passing) that still causes failure, using the oracle semantics. But we need to simulate the oracle? The task says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" But we don't have an actual oracle; we need to infer from the payload? Actually, the payload seems to already contain the answer? Look at the fixtures: they have required_difference which is the expected minimal difference? But the task says to compute the isolated difference and number of tests. The function should perform ddmin on the symmetric set difference (failing - passing) to find the minimal subset that causes failure. But we need to simulate the oracle using the given data? That seems odd.\n\nLet's re-read: \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing. Run ddmin only on this difference. Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" The payload has required_difference and unresolved_difference. Perhaps the oracle is defined by these fields: the required_difference are the minimal changes that must be present to cause failure? Or maybe the oracle is implicitly defined by the fact that any superset of required_difference causes failure, and any subset that does not include required_difference passes? But the task likely expects us to implement ddmin using a test function that checks if a given set fails based on the provided passing/failing sets? That doesn't make sense.\n\nWait, the fixture is just input shapes. The actual evaluation will provide arbitrary payloads. So we need to implement the ddmin algorithm using a test function that we can call? But we cannot access any external oracle. The only way is to assume that the oracle is given by the payload? Possibly the payload contains a flag? Or we need to treat the provided passing and failing as the full sets, and the required_difference and unresolved_difference are the expected output? The task says \"Return the isolated difference and number of tests.\" The number of tests is the number of calls to the oracle during ddmin. So we need to simulate the oracle by using the given required_difference? That is, the oracle returns PASS if the subset contains all elements of required_difference? But then why have unresolved_difference? The fixture shows unresolved_difference is empty. In real evaluation, there might be unresolved cases.\n\nMaybe the intention is that the case payload already contains the ground truth; we need to compute the ddmin result from it? But then it's trivial: the isolated difference is just the required_difference, and number of tests is something? That doesn't require any algorithm.\n\nLet's examine the candidate_artifact: it instructs to \"Implement `difference_isolation` through `solve(case)` ... Validate every required field before computing a result.\" And \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing.\" That is simply set difference. Then \"Run ddmin only on this difference.\" So we need to implement ddmin on that set, calling an oracle that determines if a subset causes failure. The oracle must be available. Where does it come from? Possibly the payload includes a method to test? No.\n\nPerhaps the missing clue: the task is SE-DD-02, likely from the \"EffectSlice\" paper or similar. The oracle is defined by the paper? In delta debugging, the oracle is a function that returns PASS, FAIL, or UNRESOLVED for a given configuration. In the fixture, the payload contains \"passing\" and \"failing\" which are lists of changes? But those are not configurations; they are the full sets of changes. The required_difference is the minimal difference that accounts for the failure. So the oracle is implicitly defined by the fact that the full failing set fails, the full passing set passes, and any superset of the minimal difference fails, and any subset that does not include all of required_difference passes. That is a typical scenario.\n\nThus, we can implement a test function that takes a subset (list of changes) and checks if it contains all elements of required_difference: if yes -> FAIL, else -> PASS. But what about UNRESOLVED? The unresolved_difference might indicate changes that are not deterministic? The spec says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" So we need to handle UNRESOLVED. If unresolved_difference is non-empty, then subsets that include some of those may be unresolved? The paper's delta debugging has three states. In the fixture unresolved_difference is empty, so we can assume only PASS/FAIL.\n\nHowever, the oracle also needs to account for the fact that the passing set passes? Actually, the oracle should be defined by the full configuration: the configuration is a combination of the base changes (passing) plus the subset from the difference. So the test should check if the configuration (passing + subset) fails. That is equivalent to checking if the subset contains the required_difference (since passing is fixed). So yes.\n\nThus, we need to implement ddmin that takes the set of changes that are in failing but not in passing (i.e., the \"change set\") and finds the minimal subset that causes failure, using a test function that returns PASS/FAIL based on whether the subset includes the required_difference. However, we don't know required_difference in advance; that's what we need to find. But the test function should be part of the problem? This is circular.\n\nWait, perhaps the payload contains a hidden test function? No.\n\nAnother interpretation: the payload gives us the actual test results? The passing and failing are the results for the full sets? No, they are lists of changes.\n\nLet's re-read the task description from the candidate_artifact: \"Isolate a minimal causal difference between passing and failing configurations under the paper's delta-debugging invariant.\" It mentions \"difference_isolation\" as a procedure. In the paper \"Yesterday, my program worked. Today, it does not. Why?\" delta debugging is used to find the minimal set of changes that cause failure given a passing and failing configuration. The required inputs are: passing configuration (list of changes), failing configuration (list of changes), and an oracle that can test any subset. The output is the minimal difference. The number of tests is the count of oracle calls.\n\nThus, in our solve function, we need to implement ddmin, but we don't have an oracle. So the oracle must be provided by the evaluator as part of the case? But the case only has those four lists. Possibly the evaluator will inject a callable into the case? But the instructions say case is exactly the inner object stored in that row's payload field. It does not include a callable.\n\nMaybe the intended solution is to treat the provided \"required_difference\" as the ground truth and compute the number of tests that would have been needed if we had an oracle? That is, we simulate ddmin and count the number of times we call the oracle, and the isolated difference is the required_difference. But that would be trivial: just count the number of steps in ddmin given the set difference and the knowledge of required_difference? That seems too contrived.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we need to implement ddmin without knowing the oracle, but the oracle is implicitly defined by the \"failing\" list: any subset that contains all elements of the symmetric difference causes failure? No.\n\nLet's look at the task scaffold: \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`. The function must return a JSON-serializable object, must not mutate `case`, and may use only the Python standard library.\" And the output schema: {isolated_difference: array of strings, tests: integer}.\n\nThe fixture payloads show three cases with different required_difference. In each, the failing set contains all c0-c2 and x0-x5, passing only c0-c2. The symmetric difference set is {x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5}. required_difference is a subset of that. For case 0: [\"x4\",\"x5\"]; case1: [\"x2\",\"x4\"]; case2: [\"x2\",\"x5\"]. So the minimal difference is not just the full difference; it's a subset.\n\nThus, the task is: Given the passing and failing sets (and perhaps additional oracle info?), compute the minimal difference using ddmin. But we don't have an oracle, so we cannot compute it without the oracle. Unless the oracle is provided by the evaluator via some hidden mechanism? Not possible.\n\nMaybe the intent is that we should use the provided \"required_difference\" as the oracle? That is, the oracle returns FAIL if the subset contains all of required_difference, else PASS. Then we run ddmin on the full difference set, and the result should be exactly required_difference, and tests is the number of calls. That seems plausible: the evaluator will give us the required_difference as part of the payload so we can simulate the oracle. But then the output is just that required_difference, and we compute tests. But the instruction says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" And \"Return the isolated difference and number of tests.\" It doesn't say to use required_difference directly; it says compute. But we are using it as oracle.\n\nHowever, the output structural schema only requires \"isolated_difference\" and \"tests\". The required_difference field in payload is not part of the output. So we can use it internally.\n\nBut is that cheating? The candidate_artifact says \"Use only quantities present in the case payload.\" So we have required_difference. It also says \"Do not call a language model, network service, random generator, wall clock, or environment-dependent resource.\" So we can use required_difference as the oracle ground truth.\n\nBut why would the evaluator provide the answer? Maybe the fixture is just an example, and the real evaluation will have hidden required_difference? No, the fixture is given to us, but the actual test cases will have different payloads with required_difference. The evaluator will call solve(case) and compare output to expected? The expected output would be the isolated_difference and tests count, which we compute using the provided required_difference as oracle. So it's consistent: the payload includes the oracle information. So we can implement ddmin with an oracle that checks if subset covers required_difference.\n\nBut wait: what about unresolved_difference? The paper's oracle can return UNRESOLVED if changes are uncertain. The fixture shows empty. But we should handle it: if a subset contains any element from unresolved_difference, the oracle should return UNRESOLVED (or maybe not? The paper defines UNRESOLVED as when the test outcome is indeterminate). In ddmin, if an oracle returns UNRESOLVED, the algorithm skips that subset or treats it differently. The standard ddmin algorithm assumes a test that returns PASS, FAIL, or UNRESOLVED. We need to implement that.\n\nThus, we need to implement ddmin as per the paper \"Yesterday, my program worked...\" The algorithm: given a set of changes C, and a test function test(C) that returns one of {PASS, FAIL, UNRESOLVED}. We want to find a minimal subset. The algorithm is:\n\ndef ddmin(c):\n    if len(c) == 1: return c\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        subsets = partition(c, n)\n        for subset in subsets:\n            if test(subset) == FAIL:\n                c = subset\n                n = 2\n                break\n            complement = [x for x in c if x not in subset]\n            if test(complement) == FAIL: # actually the paper checks if test(c - subset) fails? It checks complement\n                c = complement\n                n = 2\n                break\n        else:\n            if n < len(c):\n                n = min(2*n, len(c))\n            else:\n                break\n    return c\n\nBut there are variations. The standard ddmin algorithm (Zeller's) is:\n\ndef ddmin(c):\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        subsets = partition(c, n)\n        for subset in subsets:\n            if test(subset) == FAIL:\n                c = subset\n                n = 2\n                break\n            complement = [x for x in c if x not in subset]\n            if test(complement) == PASS: # wait, original: if test(c - subset) fails? Actually, the paper: if test(c \\ s) fails, then we reduce to c \\ s? No.\n        Let's recall: The algorithm is: For each subset, if test(s) fails, reduce to s. Otherwise, if test(c \\ s) fails, reduce to c \\ s. If neither, continue. If no reduction after all subsets, increase granularity.\n        The test on complement is also to check if it fails (i.e., failure is still present). So we need test(complement) == FAIL.\n        The algorithm also handles UNRESOLVED by treating it as not a reduction.\n\nSo we will implement that.\n\nNow, the test function will be internal, using the payload's required_difference and unresolved_difference. For a given subset (list of changes), we check if the subset contains all elements of required_difference? Actually, the test should be on the full configuration: the configuration = passing + subset. Since passing is fixed, the test outcome depends on the subset. We can define: if the subset contains all elements of required_difference, then the configuration fails. Additionally, if the subset contains any element from unresolved_difference, then the test outcome is UNRESOLVED. Otherwise, PASS. But is that correct? The unresolved_difference likely indicates changes that cause indeterministic behavior. So any subset that includes any of those should be UNRESOLVED. And required_difference are changes that are necessary for failure. So if all required_difference are present, it's FAIL; else PASS.\n\nBut note: if a subset includes both required and unresolved, what then? The paper: UNRESOLVED takes precedence? Typically, if the test is unresolved, we treat it as neither pass nor fail. So we'll return UNRESOLVED if any unresolved present. Otherwise, if all required present -> FAIL else PASS.\n\nNow, we need to handle the case where the full difference set (failing - passing) already fails? The failing set includes all required_difference, so test on full set should be FAIL. That's consistent.\n\nNow, we need to compute the number of tests: the number of calls to the test function. In ddmin, we call test on subsets and complements. We'll increment a counter each time we call test.\n\nWe must also consider ordering: \"Retain source order through every subset and complement.\" This means when partitioning the set of changes, we should keep the original order as given in the failing list? The failing list is in order: ['c0','c1','c2','x0','x1','x2','x3','x4','x5']. The difference set (failing - passing) is ['x0','x1','x2','x3','x4','x5'] maintaining order. When partitioning into subsets, we should partition based on that order (e.g., first half, second half). The algorithm should use list ordering. So we'll use list of strings, and partitions by slicing.\n\nAlso, complements should be the elements not in subset, preserving order.\n\nNow, the implementation: We'll write a solve(case) that extracts passing, failing, required_difference, unresolved_difference. Validate they are lists of strings. Then compute the set of changes that are in failing but not in passing. But we must be careful: The passing set might contain elements not in failing? The problem says \"difference between passing and failing configurations\". Typically, the difference is the set of elements that differ. We'll compute: diff = [x for x in failing if x not in passing] (preserving order). But we need to consider that passing might have elements not in failing; those are \"removed\" changes? But in delta debugging, the changes are typically a superset. The task says \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing.\" So we only consider additions. So diff = [x for x in failing if x not in passing]. That matches the fixtures.\n\nThen we define the test function using required and unresolved. Then run ddmin on diff (starting with diff). Need to handle empty diff? If diff empty, then isolated_difference = [] and tests = 0? But the problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result. Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case.\" So if required_difference is not a subset of diff? That would be invalid. Also if unresolved_difference not subset of diff? We'll check.\n\nNow, ddmin algorithm must be careful to handle cases where the test on the full set does not fail? But it should, because required_difference is subset of diff. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nWe also need to handle UNRESOLVED during ddmin. The algorithm: if test returns UNRESOLVED for a subset, we treat it as no reduction. If test returns UNRESOLVED for complement, we treat as no reduction. Then continue. If all subsets and complements give PASS/UNRESOLVED, increase granularity. If at any point we get FAIL, we reduce.\n\nWe'll implement a helper function test(subset) that returns 'PASS', 'FAIL', or 'UNRESOLVED'. We'll increment a counter.\n\nImplementation details:\n\n- Use Python standard library only.\n- No imports needed.\n- Define inner function.\n\nNow, write the solve function.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON output with \"isolated_difference\" (list of strings in order) and \"tests\" (integer).\n\nEdge cases: If diff is empty, the isolated_difference should be [] and tests=0. But the specification says \"Compute the ordered change set present in failing but absent from passing.\" If diff empty, no change. Then required_difference should be empty. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nNow, ensure we return a JSON-serializable dict.\n\nThe output envelope: whole response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\", value is the Python source code as a string. So we need to double-escape it. We'll write the code and then encode as JSON string.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1278,"completion_tokens":4822,"total_tokens":6100,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3930},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":126},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}